[2026-10-31 01:56:23] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto-Front Candidates (both objectives measured)

Filtering to experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 936.62 | 0.27 | minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.94 | 0.28 | wrinkle |
| 17p5-16deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 696.50 | 0.32 | wrinkle |
| 17-0add-5deg-30s-N2-1200s_run2 | 547.22 | 0.31 | clean |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s_run3 | 421.45 | 0.44 | minor wrinkles |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s_run5 | 468.22 | 0.40 | wrinkles |
| 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 367.39 | 0.38 | clean |
| 17p5-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.76 | 0.40 | minor fold |

The approximate Pareto front (non-dominated in modulus × pore fraction):
- **936 bar / 0.27** (run1 only, high CV=0, single specimen, wrinkles)
- **421–468 bar / 0.40–0.44** (18-15deg-600s-NoN2 cluster)
- **367 bar / 0.38** (17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2)

The 936-bar point (17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s run1) is a single-specimen outlier with SD=0 (only 1 fit survived), no independent synthesis repeat with both objectives measured. Run2 gave 675 bar / 0.30 — substantially lower modulus. Runs 4 and 5 (Oct 30) both failed to yield any modulus/pore-fraction measurements. This candidate is **unvalidated** for both objectives.

### Validation Status Check

**17p5-0add-18deg-75s-N2-1800s:**
- Run1: 936 bar / 0.27 (single fit, SD=0, minor wrinkles)
- Run2: 675 bar / 0.30 (2 fits, SD=56, wrinkles+gap)
- Run4: no modulus/pore fraction (measurement failure)
- Run5: no modulus/pore fraction (measurement failure)

The two runs with data show a large spread (936 vs 675 bar), both with quality issues. Runs 4 and 5 produced negative strain values suggesting instrument/setup problems on that campaign date. The validation policy requires an independent repeat before treating this as a confirmed Pareto improvement. However, runs 4 and 5 failed to produce usable mechanical data — they don't constitute successful validation attempts, but they do suggest this condition has reproducibility issues.

**17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s:**
- Run1: 861 bar / 0.28 (2 fits, SD=98, wrinkle)
- Run2: 549 bar / 0.30 (good quality, clean)
- Run4 (Oct 30): 673 bar / 0.27 (1 fit, SD=0, clean image!)

Run4 is from the same Oct-30 campaign as the failed runs 4/5 of the 18deg condition, but it actually produced a measurement. The three runs give moduli of 861, 549, 673 bar with pore fractions 0.28, 0.30, 0.27. Mean ~694 bar, pore fraction ~0.28. This is a strong modulus candidate with moderate pore fraction. The image quality for run4 was excellent (no defects). This condition has 3 independent synthesis runs with data — it is reasonably validated for modulus but pore fraction is consistently low (~0.27–0.30).

### Exploration Opportunity

The high-pore-fraction region (0.38–0.44) is achieved by the **18-15deg-600s-NoN2** condition and the **17p5-15deg-NoN2** conditions. These have moderate modulus (~370–470 bar). The NoN2 + longer wait time appears to promote higher pore fraction. 

An unexplored region: **higher polymer concentration (18–18.5 wt%) combined with the NoN2 + moderate wait time approach** at bath_temp ~15°C. The 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 condition achieves 0.39–0.44 pore fraction but only 340–470 bar modulus. Could a slightly different wait time or polymer concentration improve modulus while maintaining high pore fraction?

Also underexplored: **polymer_wt ~18 wt%, bath_temp ~15°C, NoN2, moderate wait ~200–300s** — this sits between the 60s-N2 experiments (lower pore fraction) and the 600s-NoN2 experiments (high pore fraction but lower modulus). The 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2 gave 367 bar / 0.38 with a clean membrane. Increasing polymer_wt to 18.0 at similar conditions might push modulus up while retaining elevated pore fraction.

### Recommendation

Explore **polymer_wt=18.0, bath_temp=15°C, NoN2, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=300s** — this is between the 200s and 400s NoN2 experiments at 17.5 wt%, but with higher polymer concentration. Higher polymer_wt tends to increase modulus (denser matrix), while the NoN2 + moderate wait time has consistently produced elevated pore fractions (0.38–0.44). This combination is physically motivated to seek a Pareto improvement: higher modulus from increased polymer concentration, maintained high pore fraction from the NoN2/moderate-wait mechanism. This specific combination has not been tested.

