### Pareto Front Review (experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured)

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 936.62 | 0.27 | minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.94 | 0.28 | wrinkle |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run2) | 549.06 | 0.30 | wrinkle |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run2) | 675.14 | 0.30 | wrinkle/gap |
| 17-15deg-90s-N2-1800s (run2) | 640.23 | 0.29 | clean |
| 17p5-25deg-375s-N2-1300s (run1) | 591.67 | 0.28 | clean |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 421.45 | **0.44** | minor wrinkles |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run5) | 468.22 | **0.40** | wrinkles |
| 17p5-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.76 | **0.40** | minor fold |
| 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 367.39 | **0.38** | clean |
| 17-10deg-60s-N2-1800s (run2) | 402.35 | 0.31 | clean |
| 18-15deg-300s-NoN2-1800s | 488.10 | 0.35 | wrinkle |

**Observed Pareto frontier pattern:**
- High modulus (700–937 bar) comes with low pore fraction (0.27–0.30): bath_temp ~18–19°C, polymer_wt 17–17.5, N2=true, short wait
- High pore fraction (0.38–0.44) comes with lower modulus (367–468 bar): NoN2, longer wait, bath_temp ~15°C
- The gap between these two clusters is large — no experiment has simultaneously achieved modulus >550 bar AND pore fraction >0.35

### Key Observations

1. **The 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s** condition (runs 1–5) shows the highest pore fractions (0.32–0.44) but with high variability (run1: 0.39, run3: 0.44, run4: 0.36, run5: 0.40) and moderate modulus (340–468 bar). Run3 is the best pore fraction observed (0.44) but has high modulus SD (120 bar).

2. **The 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s run1** has the highest modulus (937 bar) but only 1 surviving specimen (SD=0, single fit), making it unreliable. Run2 gave 675 bar / 0.30 pore fraction with wrinkles.

3. **The 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s** condition gave a clean membrane with 367 bar / 0.38 pore fraction — good quality but low modulus.

4. **The 18-15deg-300s-NoN2-1800s** gave 488 bar / 0.35 pore fraction with a wrinkle — interesting intermediate.

5. **Under-explored region**: Higher polymer_wt (18–19) combined with intermediate bath_temp (12–14°C) and N2=true with moderate wait times has not been systematically explored. Higher polymer concentration tends to increase modulus; intermediate bath temp may preserve pore fraction better than very cold baths.

### Strategy

The Pareto frontier has a gap: no condition has achieved both modulus >500 bar AND pore fraction >0.35. The data suggests:
- N2=true + cold bath → high modulus, low pore fraction
- NoN2 + longer wait → high pore fraction, lower modulus
- **Intermediate hypothesis**: polymer_wt ~18.5, bath_temp ~12–13°C, N2=true, moderate wait (~120–150s) might yield intermediate-to-high modulus while preserving more pore fraction than the very cold/short-wait conditions

This region (bath_temp 12–13°C, polymer_wt 18–18.5, N2=true, moderate wait) is genuinely under-sampled. The 18.5 wt% / 5°C / 105s / N2 condition gave 545–593 bar / 0.29–0.33 pore fraction. Moving bath_temp up to ~12°C may increase pore fraction while maintaining reasonable modulus.

Additionally, the `pullcast_speed` has been almost exclusively at 10 mm/s in recent experiments. A slightly lower speed (7–8 mm/s) with this new condition could reduce wrinkling tendency.

**Proposed experiment**: polymer_wt=18.5, bath_temp=12°C, mixing_temp=65°C, pullcast_speed=8, N2=true, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=120s, nips_bath_wait_time=1800s

This is genuinely novel (no prior experiment at bath_temp=12°C), targets the modulus-pore fraction gap, and uses a slightly reduced pullcast speed to improve membrane quality.

