[2026-10-31 16:27:43] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto-Front Candidates (both objectives measured)

Reviewing all experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 936.62 | 0.27 | minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.94 | 0.28 | wrinkle |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run2) | 675.14 | 0.30 | wrinkle+gap |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run6) | 658.85 | 0.31 | clean ✓ |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 421.45 | 0.44 | minor wrinkles |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run5) | 468.22 | 0.40 | wrinkles |
| 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 367.39 | 0.38 | clean ✓ |
| 17p5-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.76 | 0.40 | minor fold |

The Pareto front spans roughly:
- **High modulus / low pore fraction**: ~860–937 bar / 0.27–0.28 (17.5 wt%, bath 18–19°C, N2, 75–90s wait)
- **Low modulus / high pore fraction**: ~367–421 bar / 0.38–0.44 (18 wt%, bath 15°C, NoN2, long wait)

### Validation Status Check

**17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1)**: Modulus 936.62 bar is the highest observed — but this is a single-specimen fit from only 1 surviving curve (SD=0, only 1 data point), with minor wrinkling. Run2 gave 675 bar / 0.30 (wrinkle+gap), run6 gave 659 bar / 0.31 (clean). The run1 value is an outlier relative to runs 2 and 6 and is not reproducibly confirmed. Run6 is the cleanest specimen.

**17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1)**: 860.94 bar / 0.28 — single-curve fit (SD=0), wrinkle present. Run2 gave 549 bar / 0.30 (wrinkle). Run4 gave 673 bar / 0.27 (clean, but only 1 surviving curve, SD=0). The 860 bar value is not reproduced.

**18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**: Runs 1–5 show modulus 340–468 bar, pore fraction 0.32–0.44. Run3 is the best quality (minor wrinkles) with 421 bar / 0.44 — the highest pore fraction observed. This is a genuine high-pore-fraction candidate but modulus is low.

### Gap in the Pareto Front

The current Pareto front has a gap: no candidate achieves **both** modulus >500 bar **and** pore fraction >0.35 simultaneously. The high-modulus region (bath 18–19°C, N2, 17.5 wt%) gives pore fraction ~0.27–0.31. The high-pore-fraction region (bath 15°C, NoN2, long wait) gives modulus ~370–470 bar.

**Key question**: Can we find conditions that push pore fraction higher while maintaining modulus >500 bar? 

Looking at the data:
- Bath temperature is the strongest driver of pore fraction: lower bath temp → higher pore fraction (faster phase separation)
- NoN2 (longer air exposure) also increases pore fraction
- The 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 series achieves high pore fraction but low modulus
- The 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2 (clean membrane) gave 367 bar / 0.38

**Unexplored region**: Higher polymer concentration (18–18.5 wt%) combined with moderate bath temperature (14–16°C) and N2 (to maintain modulus) with moderate wait time. Higher polymer wt% tends to increase modulus; moderate bath temp (not as warm as 18–19°C) should increase pore fraction vs. the 18–19°C region.

The condition **18.0 wt%, bath 14°C, N2, 90s wait, speed 10, mixing 65°C, nips 1800s** is unexplored. Bath 14°C sits between the high-modulus zone (18–19°C) and the high-pore-fraction zone (15°C NoN2), but with N2 and slightly higher polymer wt% to support modulus. This could yield pore fraction ~0.33–0.38 with modulus potentially >500 bar — a Pareto improvement over the current front.

This is a genuinely novel point: polymer_wt=18.0 with bath_temp=14°C and N2 has not been tested. It differs from the 18-15deg-60s-N2-1800s (bath 15°C, N2, 60s) which gave 449 bar / 0.31 — going to 14°C should push pore fraction up while N2 and 18 wt% support modulus.

